1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,430
Bring back three items. It is a familiar request in games.

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:08,845
Some errands make you curious about the next destination,

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:11,190
while others just make you want to finish.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,690
The difference may lie in what happens
between the instruction and delivery.

5
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:19,710
We will compare travel and gathering in Skyrim
with resource exploration in Subnautica,

6
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:23,470
then change conditions in an original delivery prototype.

7
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:25,510
We will examine three questions.

8
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:29,125
What choices happen along the way? What changes after delivery?

9
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:32,370
And does the return journey offer anything new?

10
00:00:34,061 --> 00:00:36,773
On the beach in A Short Hike, another hiker
asks for shells.

11
00:00:37,013 --> 00:00:40,953
Watch the request and the character walking
back into the surroundings.

12
00:00:41,293 --> 00:00:44,833
Writing only the item name can leave out the
exploration in between.

13
00:00:45,073 --> 00:00:49,353
Looking nearby, climbing a hill and meeting
someone become part of the trip.

14
00:00:49,673 --> 00:00:53,453
We are looking for actions to choose, beyond
whether a marker exists.

15
00:00:53,713 --> 00:00:57,433
Visible paths and changes in height provide
information for movement.

16
00:00:57,673 --> 00:01:00,493
Every trip does not need to become a difficult
challenge.

17
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:04,053
Find one place where a small request meets the
existing exploration.

18
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:08,053
The three earlier questions help us identify
and record these real actions.

19
00:01:08,807 --> 00:01:11,777
First, look at the actions on the
way, rather than only the destination.

20
00:01:11,907 --> 00:01:14,837
When gathering materials in Subnautica,
you inspect your surroundings

21
00:01:15,107 --> 00:01:19,277
and use the remaining oxygen to decide
whether to keep searching or surface.

22
00:01:19,647 --> 00:01:21,377
This is not a quest assigned by a character,

23
00:01:21,787 --> 00:01:25,457
but an example of a player seeking materials for their own needs.

24
00:01:25,647 --> 00:01:28,872
A gathering goal can contain observation and decisions.

25
00:01:28,887 --> 00:01:32,237
If following a marker leaves no action or route to choose,

26
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:36,292
extra travel time may add waiting rather than meaningful activity.

27
00:01:36,307 --> 00:01:40,677
Write down the moments when players
make decisions during your quest.

28
00:01:40,927 --> 00:01:44,537
Check whether those choices actually exist between destinations.

29
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,340
While climbing, we can see height and the
remaining feathers together.

30
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:53,180
There can be a moment to consider continuing
upward or taking another direction.

31
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:58,180
Here, observe the position reached and the
change in the feather indicator.

32
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:01,900
When the scene shifts to gliding, the route no
longer follows only ground paths.

33
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:06,140
The hills and waterfront below become possible
places to land.

34
00:02:06,440 --> 00:02:10,200
Walking and flying involve different
movements, even toward a similar destination.

35
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:13,580
These excerpts are not a numeric experiment
ranking the routes for fun.

36
00:02:13,583 --> 00:02:17,360
Look for choices that the movement method
leaves in your own quest.

37
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:21,420
Decisions along the way turn the time between
destinations into play.

38
00:02:22,423 --> 00:02:25,273
Next, examine what changes when the task is completed.

39
00:02:25,443 --> 00:02:28,293
Here are travel and mining scenes from Skyrim.

40
00:02:28,423 --> 00:02:32,053
This footage does not demonstrate the reward of a particular quest.

41
00:02:32,263 --> 00:02:35,253
It illustrates the actions of travel and gathering.

42
00:02:35,463 --> 00:02:38,133
The question for our own game is separate.

43
00:02:38,283 --> 00:02:40,973
After delivering the items, does only the score increase?

44
00:02:41,203 --> 00:02:44,033
Or does a route open, or someone behave differently?

45
00:02:44,163 --> 00:02:48,233
A numerical reward can matter, but a visible consequence

46
00:02:48,643 --> 00:02:51,913
may help players understand why the task mattered.

47
00:02:52,423 --> 00:02:55,353
We will build that difference in a small delivery game.

48
00:02:56,017 --> 00:03:00,097
Watch the compass acquisition message that
appears after a conversation.

49
00:03:00,537 --> 00:03:03,897
Receiving the item is clearly observable on
the screen.

50
00:03:04,237 --> 00:03:08,357
The next question is how that item might be
used afterward.

51
00:03:08,637 --> 00:03:12,017
In the earlier bridge test, completion changed
where we could walk.

52
00:03:12,437 --> 00:03:16,357
For an item, record what information or
actions may be available in the next search.

53
00:03:16,577 --> 00:03:20,477
Separate opening a treasure chest from the
movement that follows the acquisition.

54
00:03:20,877 --> 00:03:24,997
Spiritfarer shows gathering and material
processing as other linked actions.

55
00:03:24,997 --> 00:03:29,417
Think beyond the goal of collecting an item to
its later use.

56
00:03:29,717 --> 00:03:32,957
Check the acquisition number and the change in
the next action separately.

57
00:03:33,707 --> 00:03:37,297
Both versions ask you to collect three
logs along the same route and return.

58
00:03:37,587 --> 00:03:40,117
Movement speed, item positions, and the score reward are identical.

59
00:03:40,667 --> 00:03:43,917
In the first version, delivery raises
the score and completes the request.

60
00:03:44,267 --> 00:03:47,317
In the second, the same delivery repairs a broken bridge.

61
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:50,497
The places you can reach afterward actually change.

62
00:03:50,827 --> 00:03:53,937
The bridge is more than decorative completion artwork.

63
00:03:54,147 --> 00:03:57,637
A blocked collision area opens, allowing the character to cross.

64
00:03:58,107 --> 00:04:01,292
This comparison demonstrates a change in the world state.

65
00:04:01,307 --> 00:04:05,337
Whether the second version is more
enjoyable still needs human playtesting.

66
00:04:05,727 --> 00:04:08,837
A successful automated input trace is not an enjoyment rating.

67
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:13,880
In Spiritfarer, moving around the ship brings
us to different work areas.

68
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:17,940
Watch material handling and caring for
passengers in sequence.

69
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:21,820
Both involve an interaction, but their visible
results differ.

70
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:26,720
For materials, observe what is processed; for
care, observe who responds.

71
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:30,860
Our earlier test changed only the bridge state
after the same wood delivery.

72
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:34,960
These different game excerpts are not two
versions of that controlled comparison.

73
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:39,140
They help us look for the target and next
action connected by a request.

74
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:43,760
We can record a usable item or a relationship
response as well as an opening door.

75
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:47,200
Show the completion message together with the
result that remains.

76
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:49,730
Now examine the return journey.

77
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:54,630
You initially take a detour, but repairing
the bridge opens a shorter route.

78
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,690
The same location now has a different role.

79
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:02,510
Another test adds a gate on the short
route that requires an empty inventory.

80
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:06,850
You choose between returning quickly and
carrying the item along the longer route.

81
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:10,330
The presence of a shortcut alone does not create a decision.

82
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,690
Players need to understand what they gain and give up.

83
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:17,165
You do not need an event during every moment of travel.

84
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:22,110
Quiet time can allow players to notice the surroundings or reflect.

85
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:25,530
First decide what purpose a repeated stretch should serve.

86
00:05:26,267 --> 00:05:29,767
Return to the gliding and return travel in A
Short Hike.

87
00:05:30,087 --> 00:05:33,727
Coming down from a high place shows familiar
scenery from another direction.

88
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:37,987
A place seen earlier may provide a clue for
choosing where to go next.

89
00:05:38,287 --> 00:05:42,567
Our original test changed the return route
through the bridge and inventory conditions.

90
00:05:42,907 --> 00:05:46,667
In the real example, observe where the
character looks and moves downward.

91
00:05:46,967 --> 00:05:50,767
This does not mean adding a new enemy or event
to every return trip.

92
00:05:51,027 --> 00:05:55,467
Spiritfarer also shows movement around the
ship between work areas and passengers.

93
00:05:55,467 --> 00:06:00,027
Decide whether the end of a trip leads to
another task or leaves room to rest.

94
00:06:00,387 --> 00:06:04,327
The same place can be revisited with a
different goal and different information.

95
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:08,990
When designing a quest, add three lines beneath its objective.

96
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:13,410
List choices on the way, changes after
the task, and new information on return.

97
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:16,310
If all three are empty, before adding more requests,

98
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:19,810
introduce a small change in one stretch of the journey.

99
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:24,570
Ask players where they paused and which route they chose.

100
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:28,410
Check what they thought changed after completion.

101
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:31,805
Their understanding may differ from your design intent.

102
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:35,190
The number of items alone does not determine a good errand.

103
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:38,170
Examine the decisions players make inside a small request

104
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:40,970
alongside the changes those decisions leave behind.

105
00:06:41,167 --> 00:06:45,707
Finally, record one request from real scenes
in three parts.

106
00:06:46,007 --> 00:06:50,547
First, note the route and action chosen after
hearing the request and setting out.

107
00:06:50,767 --> 00:06:54,667
Next, note the visible result of finding or
using an item.

108
00:06:54,907 --> 00:06:58,767
Last, note the new place or next task noticed
while returning.

109
00:06:58,987 --> 00:07:02,887
A record of character movement differs from
evidence that a player understood it.

110
00:07:03,327 --> 00:07:07,247
Ask someone who played which choices and
changes they remember.

111
00:07:07,247 --> 00:07:11,707
If those are unclear, inspect the delivery
scene before increasing the item count.

112
00:07:12,107 --> 00:07:16,347
Add one observed action and result to each of
the three earlier questions.

113
00:07:16,647 --> 00:07:20,027
A small errand becomes material for designing
the play in between.
